(10028, 'Welcome!  Welcome to Yebb Neblegear''s Darkmoon Zoo Bizarre - a Trove of Living Legends and Mythical Oddities!  Enjoy your visit, friend!$B$BBut be mindful of what you touch!  These exhibits hold artifacts and beasts both dire and divine!  They might burn the wayward hand of an over-curious onlooker...', '', 0, 0, 0, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
(10029, 'Where can I send you today $c?', '', 0, 0, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
(10030, 'Though the Crossroads seems to be the center of activity in The Barrens, I prefer the quietude of Camp Taurajo.  Where shall I send you?', '', 0, 0, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
(10031, 'Many are the paths of the Earth Mother.  Wherever you decide to travel, may she watch over you.', '', 0, 0, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
(10032, 'Treat the wind rider well as it takes you to your destination.  May your ancestors watch over you $n.', '', 0, 0, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
(10033, 'Greetings my friend, and welcome to the greatest show on Azeroth!$B$BPlease, step right up and take in all we have to offer.  Amaze at the wonders that the Darkmoon Faire has uncovered in this vast and mysterious world!  We have spared no expense in bringing you excitement that children of all ages will delight in!', '', 0, 0, 0, 66, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0),
(10034, 'Welcome back to the greatest show on Azeroth, my friend!  It is truly a delight to see you once again.$B$BPlease, step right up and once more take in all we have to offer.  Be sure to check around, as we are always adding new and exciting things to the Darkmoon Faire!  Amaze at wonders old and new that we have uncovered!  We continue to spare no expense in bringing you excitement that children of all ages will delight in!', '', 0, 0, 0, 2, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0),
(10035, 'Silas, why is most everything at the fair free?  How do you make a profit?', 'Silas, why is most everything at the fair free?  How do you make a profit?', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
(10036, 'It''s not always about money, clem!  While we receive generous donations - some extremely substantial - our traveling troupe of performers is fueled creatively by a need to brighten this war-torn world.  Don''t worry about us, but instead let us worry about how to best entertain you.', '', 0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0),
(10037, 'Ticket redemption here!  Get your tickets turned into prizes!  Step right up!', '', 0, 0, 0, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
(10038, 'Move along, rube.', '', 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
(10039, 'This ain''t a ride, clem.  Beat it.', '', 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
(10040, 'If you''re trying to be friendly, save your breath.  Better yet, just stop breathing all together.', '', 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
(10041, 'In case it wasn''t obvious, I''m not here for customer relations.', '', 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
(10042, 'You mess with Silas, you mess with me.  Have a nice day.', '', 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
(10043, 'I need a second head like I need to lose my first one.', '', 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
(10044, 'I lived in Dire Maul for a little bit.  That was fun.  Really.', '', 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
(10045, 'Unlike most ogres, I do know what an outhouse is for - giving you a swirlie.  Now beat it.', '', 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
(10046, 'I have long known you''d seek me out, $N.$B$BEvery sentient being in this world is driven by the choices they make.  Choices begat further choices, and these in turn make someone who they are.  This is where your fortune is drawn from - who you are, and the choices you make.$B$BAre you ready to discover where your fortune lies?', '', 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
(10047, 'I am ready to discover where my fortune lies!', 'I am ready to discover where my fortune lies!', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
(10048, 'Very well then...$B$BYou have been tasked by your liege to guard his fields of corn from poachers and thieves.  One night on patrol, you stumble across a haggard man in thread-bare clothing stealing corn from the field.  You quickly confront him, and he immediately begs for his life.  He claims he is stealing the corn to feed his family since the lord of the land - your liege - demands too much in taxes.  Your liege is indeed known for his harsh taxes throughout the land.$B$BMake your choice.
', '', 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
(10049, 'I slay the man on the spot as my liege would expect me to do, as he is nothing more than a thief and a liar.', 'I slay the man on the spot as my liege would expect me to do, as he is nothing more than a thief and a liar.', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
(10050, 'I turn over the man to my liege for punishment, as he has broken the law of the land and it is my sworn duty to enforce it.', 'I turn over the man to my liege for punishment, as he has broken the law of the land and it is my sworn duty to enforce it.', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
(10051, 'I confiscate the corn he has stolen, warn him that stealing is a path towards doom and destruction, but I let him go to return to his family.', 'I confiscate the corn he has stolen, warn him that stealing is a path towards doom and destruction, but I let him go to return to his family.', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
(10052, 'I allow the man to take enough corn to feed his family for a couple of days, encouraging him to leave the land.', 'I allow the man to take enough corn to feed his family for a couple of days, encouraging him to leave the land.', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
(10053, 'Your next choice awaits.$B$BYou are the sworn executioner for your liege.  A man has been sent to you for execution as a traitor of the liege and your people.  You know this individual as a close, virtuous friend who mysteriously vanished one day.  You also know that the man''s crimes for which he was convicted supposedly caused the deaths of many innocent civilians.  Upon seeing you he begs for his life, claiming he was framed by a higher authority.$B$BMake your choice.

', '', 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
(10054, '', 'Begone, uncouth scum!  The Alliance shall prevail in Alterac Valley!', 6, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
(10055, 'Die!  Your kind has no place in Alterac Valley!', '', 6, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
(10056, '', 'Take heart, Alliance!  Throw these villains from Alterac Valley!', 6, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
(10057, 'Now is the time to attack!  For the Horde!', '', 6, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
(10058, 'Can''t you see I''ve got work to do here?', 'Can''t you see I''ve got work to do here?', 0, 0, 0, 6, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
(10059, 'Faire''s a coming!', 'Faire''s a coming!', 0, 0, 0, 71, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
(10060, 'That''s right friend!  The Darkmoon Faire is going to be right here.  Just as soon as I unload those huge wagons, put up all the tents, erect the zoo and pour the drinks.  Make sure you come back!', 'That''s right friend!  The Darkmoon Faire is going to be right here.  Just as soon as I unload those huge wagons, put up all the tents, erect the zoo and pour the drinks.  Make sure you come back!', 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
(10061, 'Won''t be long now until the Darkmoon Faire opens.  Come back later and check to see if we''re done.', 'Won''t be long now until the Darkmoon Faire opens.  Come back later and check to see if we''re done.', 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
(10062, 'Welcome to the Faire young one!   We have collected wonders from all over the world for your perusal.   Oh and please let me know if you manage to collect a set of the lesser darkmoon cards, as the master...I mean I would be keenly interested in them.', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0) ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

', 'I execute him as per my liege''s instructions, but doing so in as painless of a way as possible.  Justice must be served, but I can show some compassion.
', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
(10077, 'I risk my own life and free him so that he may prove his innocence.  If I can, I''ll help him in any way.', 'I risk my own life and free him so that he may prove his innocence.  If I can, I''ll help him in any way.', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
(10078, 'Your next choice awaits.$B$BYou are on a diplomatic mission for your liege to a cruel country that is currently at war with yours.  The war has been costly on both sides.  Your mission is to secure a peace that is honorable to both peoples.$B$BAt a diplomatic dinner, you overhear the ruler of the opposing land viciously insult your benevolent liege.  You are fairly certain the ruler meant to say it out of your earshot, but nonetheless he has besmirched your liege''s honor.$B$BMake your choice.


', '', 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
(10079, 'I confront the ruler on his malicious behavior, upholding my liege''s honor at the risk of any future diplomacy.', 'I confront the ruler on his malicious behavior, upholding my liege''s honor at the risk of any future diplomacy.', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
(10080, 'I not-so-quietly ignore the insult, hoping to instill a fear in the ruler that he may have gaffed.  I then inform my liege of the insult when I return.', 'I not-so-quietly ignore the insult, hoping to instill a fear in the ruler that he may have gaffed.  I then inform my liege of the insult when I return.', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
(10081, 'I quietly ignore the insult.  I will not tell my liege, as I am to secure peace at all costs.  It''s only an insult - not a declaration of war.', 'I quietly ignore the insult.  I will not tell my liege, as I am to secure peace at all costs.  It''s only an insult - not a declaration of war.', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
(10082, 'Your next choice awaits.$B$BYour brother seeks to join the ranks of the prestigious military order you are already a part of.  While you are sure of his commitment and loyalty, his talent and ability to perform the arduous tasks of the order is what you question.  While you are not certain he will be able to rise to the challenge of the order - putting you and others in danger - you are certain that your word alone would allow him in or keep him out.$B$BMake your choice.

', '', 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
(10083, 'I would speak against my brother joining the order, rushing a permanent breech in our relationship.  He would be a danger to himself and those around him, and that is too great a risk hoping he would improve over time.', 'I would speak against my brother joining the order, rushing a permanent breech in our relationship.  He would be a danger to himself and those around him, and that is too great a risk hoping he would improve over time.', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
(10084, 'I would speak for my brother joining the order, potentially risking the safety of the order.  I could help him with the order''s regimens, and I''d have faith in his ability to adapt and learn.', 'I would speak for my brother joining the order, potentially risking the safety of the order.  I could help him with the order''s regimens, and I''d have faith in his ability to adapt and learn.', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
(10085, 'I would create some surreptitious means to keep my brother out of the order.  I can keep him out without him being any bit wiser, thereby saving our familial bonds.', 'I would create some surreptitious means to keep my brother out of the order.  I can keep him out without him being any bit wiser, thereby saving our familial bonds.', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
(10086, 'Your next choice awaits.$B$BYou alone have defeated a terrible beast that has been ravaging the countryside, taking its ear as a trophy.  You later learn that your liege had offered a reward for the beast''s death, and that a kind but destitute knight who you know is trying to support a family by meager means claims the beast''s kill as his own.  You have no real need for money yourself, but you know that the destitute knight is lying for his own personal gain.$B$BMake your choice.

', '', 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0),
(10087, 'I would show my liege the beast''s ear and claim the beast''s death as my own, taking the reward for my own use.  It is wrong to claim a deed as your own that someone else in fact did.
', 'I would show my liege the beast''s ear and claim the beast''s death as my own, taking the reward for my own use.  It is wrong to claim a deed as your own that someone else in fact did.
